<Layout pageTitle="Cortical recording lab guide" subtitle="Controls, experiments, and interpretation."><article><p><a href="/simulations/cortex-slab/">Open the lab →</a></p><h2>Compare recording geometries</h2><ol><li>Choose Single contact, Depth probe, or 2 × 2 array.</li><li>Rotate with left-drag, pan with right-drag, and zoom with the wheel. Top/Side view buttons provide repeatable viewpoints.</li><li>Select an electrode with Prev/Next. Set its coordinates or click the slab; Shift-click also sets depth.</li><li>Vary noise drive, pause, and inspect the two-second raster and traces. Moving a contact clears old recordings.</li><li>Export raw CSV to save every contact's unfiltered samples at 8 kHz. Restart recording repeats the seeded model with the current parameters and contacts.</li></ol><h2>Interpretation</h2><p>The raster uses known synthetic source events, selected by an expected isolated peak of at least 6 µV. It is not a detector applied to the noisy trace. The trace reference line is display-only. RMS describes the selected unfiltered two-second buffer; initially it includes empty prehistory.</p><h2>Model</h2><p>There are 1,600 seeded point neurons, stochastic spike events, local recruitment, distance-weighted spike waveforms, and correlated background noise. Fixed 4 ms time steps carry waveform tails into the next block. Signal units are synthetic µV and are not calibrated to biological recordings. The model omits layered conductivity, membrane biophysics, impedance, and a physical reference electrode.</p></article></Layout>
